course: everyday
lesson: 300
title: Campfires, rivers, beaches, pools, floods and rainbows
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Fire and water give us lots of fun outdoors, and both can hurt quickly. Most accidents happen in a moment: a trip near a fire, a slip on wet stones, a push into a pool.
explain: Around a campfire, stay outside a safe circle, about three big steps away, and never run near it. For a small burn, cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell a grown-up; ice or butter make burns worse.
explain: Water is heavier than it looks. Fast floodwater only as deep as your ankle and shin (about 15 cm or 6 inches) can knock a person over, and water about 30 cm (one foot) deep can float a car. "Turn around, don't drown."
explain: Example: a girl in Florida at a friend's house sees the pool on a hot afternoon, but the grown-ups are inside. She waits, because most child drownings happen in home pools when no one is watching, and drowning is quick and quiet.
explain: A rainbow forms when sunlight bends and splits inside raindrops. To see one, stand with the Sun behind you and look towards the rain. A rainbow is not a thing in one place, so you can never reach its end.
fact: Stay about three big steps away from a campfire and never run around it, because you can trip and fall in.
fact: Roast marshmallows with a long stick or fork, at the side of the flames, and blow the marshmallow out if it catches fire.
fact: Burning marshmallow sticks to skin and burns badly.
fact: Cool a small burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell a grown-up; ice or butter make it worse.
fact: Only cross a river where there is a bridge, or with a grown-up who says it is safe; wet stones and logs are slippery.
fact: Cross on the bridge, not on its railings, and never jump off a bridge into water, because there may be rocks or shallow water.
fact: Take all your litter home from the beach, including bottle caps and small bits of plastic, because plastic in the sea kills turtles, birds and fish.
fact: Walk, never run, around a pool, because the wet floor is slippery and falls onto hard tiles cause head injuries.
fact: Never push, dunk or hold anyone under water, even in fun; people can hit the side, swallow water and drown.
fact: Never go in or near a pool without a grown-up watching; most child drownings happen in home pools when no one is watching.
fact: If floodwater covers a road or path, turn around; never walk, ride or drive through it.
fact: About 15 cm (6 inches) of moving water can knock a person down, and about 30 cm (1 foot) can float a car.
fact: To see a rainbow, stand with the Sun behind you and look towards the rain; you can never reach its end, because the rainbow moves as you move.
exercise: Your family lights a campfire. Where should you sit? | Outside a safe circle, about three big steps away | Right next to it to keep warm | As close as you like, as long as you are careful and keep still | On the stones around the fire
exercise: Why should you never run around a campfire? | You can trip and fall into the fire | Running makes the fire go out and spoils the cooking | Smoke follows people who run | Running scares away the stars
exercise: How should you roast a marshmallow? | On a long stick, at the side of the flames | On a short stick, right in the middle of the flames | Holding it in your fingers over the fire | Dropped on the hot coals
exercise: Your marshmallow catches fire. What should you do? | Blow it out, keeping the stick still | Wave the stick around fast in the air to put it out | Grab it to put it out | Eat it quickly while it burns
exercise: A spark burns the back of your hand. What should you do? | Cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell a grown-up | Rub butter on it to soothe it | Put ice straight on it | Cover it up tightly and say nothing, so nobody fusses about it
exercise: On a walk you reach a stream with a fallen log across it. What is right? | Cross only at a bridge, or with a grown-up who says it is safe | Run across the log quickly before it has time to wobble or slip | Hop across on the wet stones | Crawl across the log by yourself
exercise: Some older children are jumping off a bridge into a river. What should you do? | Cross on the bridge and never jump off | Jump only from the lowest part | Climb onto the railings to watch | Jump feet first, which is always safe in a river
exercise: Why is jumping off bridges into water dangerous? | There can be hidden rocks or shallow water | The water is always too warm and makes you feel ill | Bridges are slippery when dry | Fish can bite jumpers
exercise: At the end of a beach day you have bottle caps and wrappers. What should you do? | Take all your litter home | Bury it deep in the warm sand | Leave it by the bin if it is full | Throw small bits into the sea
exercise: Why is plastic on the beach a problem? | Plastic in the sea kills turtles, birds and fish | Plastic melts in the sun and slowly turns back into sand | Plastic makes the waves bigger | Plastic turns the sea green
exercise: You are late for your swimming lesson at the pool. What should you do? | Walk, never run, around the pool | Run quickly but carefully so you're not late | Run lightly on your tiptoes instead | Slide along the wet floor to save time
exercise: Your friend thinks it would be funny to hold you under the water. What is true? | Nobody should ever be held under water, even in fun | It is fine for a few seconds | It is fine if you are a good swimmer and hold your breath | It is fine in the shallow end
exercise: At a holiday villa the pool gate is open but the grown-ups are inside. What should you do? | Stay away from the pool until a grown-up is watching | Swim only in the shallow end, where you can stand up | Sit on the edge with your feet in | Jump in once and get out quickly
exercise: After a storm, water is flowing across the road on your way home. What should you do? | Turn around and find another way | Walk through it if it is below your knees | Ride your bike through it really fast | Wade through holding a friend's hand
exercise: How much moving water can knock a person down? | About 15 cm, around ankle to shin deep | At least one metre | Only water above your head | Only water that is very cold and fast like a waterfall
exercise: Where should you look to see a rainbow? | Towards the rain, with the Sun behind you | Straight at the Sun | Down at the ground below you | Up at the clear blue sky, far away from any rain or clouds
test: A boy in Ontario is toasting a marshmallow and his little sister wants to play tag around the fire. What should he say? | No running near the fire; we could fall in | Only run on the far side of the fire | Run quickly round the fire so the smoke cannot follow us | It is fine if we hold hands
test: Which first aid for a burn is right? | Cool running water for 20 minutes | Ice cubes held on the burn for an hour | Butter or cooking oil rubbed in gently | Blowing on it hard for a minute or two
test: A family in Wales wants to cross a fast stream on stepping stones. Why is this risky? | Wet stones are slippery and a fall into a river is dangerous | Stepping stones always sink | The stones get too hot in the sun and burn your feet | Fish nest under the stones
test: What would happen to a plastic bottle cap left on the beach? | It could be washed into the sea and eaten by a bird or turtle | It would turn into sand by morning and harm nothing | It would dissolve safely in the water | It would be eaten by crabs and do no harm
test: Why do lifeguards blow the whistle when children run around a pool? | Wet tiles are slippery and falls cause head injuries | Running makes the water in the pool cold for the swimmers | Running uses up the pool's air | Running splashes the lifeguard
test: Where do most young children drown? | In home pools when no one is watching | Only in the deep sea, far from the beach | Only in big public pools with lifeguards | Only in winter, when the water is cold
test: A car in Arizona drives into a flooded dip in the road. What could happen? | Water about 30 cm deep could float the car away | The car is too heavy to move, so it is perfectly safe | The car would always drive out safely | The water would just clean the tyres
test: A girl runs towards a rainbow to find the end. What will happen? | She can never reach it, because it moves as she moves | She will find it at the next hill | She will find the end in the rain | She can touch it if she runs fast enough to get there before it fades
test: my dad reckons put butter on a burn, true? | No, cool running water for 20 minutes | Yes, butter cools it down fast | Yes, or put ice on it | No, use toothpaste instead
test: roads flooded, only like ankle deep, we'll ride thru | No, turn around, moving water can knock you down | Yes, ankle deep is always safe on a bike | Yes, ride fast so you don't fall | Walk your bike through instead
test: gonna dunk my cousin in the pool as a joke | No, never hold anyone under, people can drown | Fine, if it's only for a second or two | Fine in the shallow end | Fine if he's a good swimmer
test: rainbows out!! which way do i look, sun's behind me | Towards the rain, with the Sun behind you | Straight at the Sun | Behind you, towards the Sun | Down at the ground
explain: A boy in Canada ran around the campfire playing tag and tripped; his hand landed in the embers and he needed treatment for burns for weeks.
explain: Two teenagers in Ireland jumped off a bridge into a river on a hot day; the cold water shocked one of them, and he drowned before his friend could reach him.
fact: Cold water can make the body gasp and muscles stop working, even in strong swimmers.
fact: Rivers can be deep and fast just beneath a calm surface.
test: mates jumping off the bridge into the river, wanna go | No, cold water and hidden rocks kill people every year | Yes, if they're fine | Yes, if you're a good swimmer | Yes, feet first
test: flooded road, water looks shallow, walk through? | No, floodwater can be deep and fast | Yes, if it only comes up under your knees | Yes, if you hold hands with a friend | Yes, if a car just went through it fine
test: running round the campfire playing tag | Stop, walk and stay three big steps away | Run faster, then the sparks can't catch you | Run only on one side | Run if the fire is small
test: did u hear?? a kid at the caravan park tried to walk through the floodwater on the road and "got swept right down the drain". lesson? | Never walk, ride or drive through floodwater; turn around | Shallow-looking floodwater is always fine to walk through | Only cars get swept away | Hold your breath and run across
test: my uncle says a boy dunked his mate at the pool "for like a whole minute" and "he had to be brought back to life". what's the true bit | Never hold anyone under water, even in fun | Dunking is safe as long as it's quick | Strong swimmers can't drown | Only deep pools are dangerous
test: ppl say the girl who fell in the campfire was "running around like an idiot". pass it on? | No, don't spread it; just stay three steps back and never run there | Yes, she was an idiot | Yes, and tell everyone how bad her burns are and where they are | Yes, it's a warning to all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the depth of moving floodwater sufficient to knock a person down? | Approximately fifteen centimetres, or six inches | Approximately one metre | Approximately two metres | Floodwater is unable to knock a grown person down at any depth
exam: Identify the principal reason for which persons walk, and do not run, around a swimming pool. | The wet floor is slippery and falls onto hard tiles cause head injuries | Running disturbs the swimmers in the water | Running warms the water | The lifeguard requires a clear view of the clock
exam: It is given that a person wishes to see a rainbow. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The Sun shall be behind them while they look towards the rain | They shall face the Sun directly during the heaviest rainfall | They shall stand within the rain cloud | They shall walk towards the rainbow's end
exam: State the correct manner of crossing a river. | By a bridge, or with an adult who confirms it is safe | By jumping between wet stones | By walking along the bridge railings | By wading across where the surface appears calm and shallow
